#{[17592186345138 "The matrix $D$ is given below. Let ${d}_{1}$, ${d}_{2}$, and ${d}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $D.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(D).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${d}_{3}={x}_{1}{d}_{1}+{x}_{2}{d}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -7 & -17\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186345139 "The matrix $P$ is given below. Let ${p}_{1}$, ${p}_{2}$, and ${p}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $P.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(P).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${p}_{3}={x}_{1}{p}_{1}+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186345143 "The matrix $W$ is given below. Let ${w}_{1}$, ${w}_{2}$, and ${w}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $W.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(W).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${w}_{2}={x}_{1}{w}_{1}+{x}_{3}{w}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nW=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186345149 "The matrix $H$ is given below. Let ${h}_{1}$, ${h}_{2}$,  ${h}_{3}$ and ${h}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $H.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{h}_{1},{h}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(H).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${h}_{4}={x}_{1}{h}_{1}+{x}_{2}{h}_{2}.$ \n\n$$\nH=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 12 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186345151 "The matrix $V$ is given below. Let ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$,  ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $V.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${v}_{2}={x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nV=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186351226 "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{2}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|?$\n\n$$\nU=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$"] [17592186351227 "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{2}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|?$\n\n$$\nU={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$"] [17592186351228 "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$\n\n$$\nU={\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$"] [17592186351544 "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$"] [17592186351545 "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}?$\n\n$$\nU={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$"] [17592186351549 "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$\n\n$$\nU={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$"] [17592186360505 "Let $K$ be the set of all $2\\times 1$ matrices $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},$ such that $x,y\\in R$ and $x+y=1.$  Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$K$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to matrix addition and scalar multiplication) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*"] [17592186360532 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*Given any $x\\in V$, if $w$ is a vector such that $x+w=0$, then $w=-x$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =0+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =(-x)+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,] \\\\ w & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186364351 "If $r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $s=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$, then $r\\cdot s=$"] [17592186366822 "Let\n$r=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ z \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $s=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ z+1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ z \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf $r\\cdot s=1$, find the value of $z.$"] [17592186368723 "Let\n$l=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ y \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $m=\\begin{bmatrix}y+1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf $l\\cdot m=0$, find the value of $y.$"] [17592186370894 "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$"] [17592186371119 "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{2}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|?$\n\n$$\nU={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$"] [17592186382854 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*If $u$ and $v$ are vectors such that $u+x=v+x$ for some $x\\in V,$ then $u=v.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+x & =v+x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (u+x)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ u+(x+(-x)) & =v+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ u+0 & =v+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ u & =v &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186385324 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Given any $x\\in V$, if $w$ is a vector such that $x+w=0$, then $w=-x$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =0+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =(-x)+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A1}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186385831 "Let $Y$ be the set of all ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$  such that $x+y\\le 3.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$Y$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to the usual component-wise addition and scalar multiplication in ${R}^{2}$) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*"] [17592186386120 "Let $v=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $4v+2w.$"] [17592186391769 "Let $c=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $d=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $c+d.$"] [17592186391776 "If $b=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$, then $-\\frac{1}{2}b=$"] [17592186391783 "If $c=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}$, then $-\\frac{1}{4}c=$"] [17592186396592 "Let $Y$ be the set of all polynomials in the variable $x\\in R$ with coefficients in $R$ of degree exactly $2.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$Y$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*"] [17592186396599 "Let $L$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ of the form $f(x)=ax+1,$ where $a\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$L$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*"] [17592186396606 "Let $I$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ of the form $f(x)={2}^{ax},$ where $a\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$I$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*"] [17592186396722 "Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials in the real  variable $x$ with coefficients in $Q.$ Complete the following sentence: \n\n*$S$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*"] [17592186399587 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*$0\\cdot 0=0$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\cdot 0 & =0\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =0\\cdot 0+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =(0+1)\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A7}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =1\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{addition of integers}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A5}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186402362 "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -10 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186402389 "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186402434 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*$0x=0$ for any $x\\in V$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (0+0)x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{property of number }0\\,] \\\\ 0x+0x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ (0x+0x)+(-0x) & =0x+(-0x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-0x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (0x+0x)+(-0x) & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ 0x+(0x+(-0x)) & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ 0x+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ 0x & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186423621 "The matrix $F$ is given below. Let ${f}_{1}$, ${f}_{2}$,  ${f}_{3}$ and ${f}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $F.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(F).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${f}_{4}={x}_{1}{f}_{1}+{x}_{2}{f}_{2}+{x}_{3}{f}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -4 & -24 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186423670 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*If $u$ and $v$ are vectors such that $x+u=x+v$ for some $x\\in V,$ then $u=v.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+u & =x+v &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+u)+(-x) & =(x+v)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (u+x)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ u+(x+(-x)) & =v+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ u+0 & =v+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ u & =v &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186426072 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186435356 "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$, and ${q}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${q}_{3}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186435357 "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$,  ${q}_{3}$, and ${q}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${q}_{4}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186435358 "The matrix $W$ is given below. Let ${w}_{1}$, ${w}_{2}$, and ${w}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $W.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(W).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${w}_{2}={x}_{1}{w}_{1}+{x}_{3}{w}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nW=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -9 & 6 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186435676 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and  $det(A)=1?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186442802 "The matrix $H$ is given below. Let ${h}_{1}$, ${h}_{2}$,  ${h}_{3}$ and ${h}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $H.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{h}_{1},{h}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(H).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${h}_{4}={x}_{1}{h}_{1}+{x}_{3}{h}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nH=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 18 & -3 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186442957 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*$\\alpha 0=0$ for any $\\alpha \\in R$*\n\n*Proof:* \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0 & =\\alpha (0+0) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0+\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0) & =(\\alpha 0+\\alpha 0)+(-\\alpha 0) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-\\alpha 0\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0) & =\\alpha 0+(\\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =\\alpha 0+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0 & =\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186446374 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and  $det(A)=0?$ \n\nI. $A$ is not invertible\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5$\n\nIII. $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$"] [17592186446376 "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{WW}} &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?"] [17592186446377 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$ .*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $det(A)=0$\n\nII. $\\text{Null}(A)={R}^{6}$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=6$"] [17592186446378 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A)<n,$ then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ is invertible\n\nII. $\\text{Null}(A)$ has no basis\n\nIII. $det(A)=0$"] [17592186446380 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then  $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ is invertible\n\nII. $det(A)=0$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=n$"] [17592186484219 "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$, and ${q}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${q}_{3}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186484246 "The matrix $P$ is given below. Let ${p}_{1}$, ${p}_{2}$,  ${p}_{3}$ and ${p}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $P.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(P).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${p}_{4}={x}_{1}{p}_{1}+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186484287 "The matrix $H$ is given below. Let ${h}_{1}$, ${h}_{2}$, and ${h}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $H.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{h}_{1},{h}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(H).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${h}_{2}={x}_{1}{h}_{1}+{x}_{3}{h}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nH=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & -8 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186485510 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*If $w$ and $v$ are vectors such that $x+w=v+x$ for some $x\\in V$, then $w=v.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =v+x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =v+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =v+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w & =v &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186500329 "The matrix $V$ is given below. Let ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$,  ${v}_{3}$, and ${v}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $V.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${v}_{4}={x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nV=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186529139 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and  $det(A)=-1?$ \n\nI. $A$ is **not** invertible\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n\nIII. $A$ has $4$ pivot columns"] [17592186536836 "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$,  ${q}_{3}$ and ${q}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{4}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{4}$, where ${q}_{3}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{4}{q}_{4}.$ \n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & -30 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 24 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186594681 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*$0\\cdot 0=0$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\cdot 0 & =0\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =0\\cdot 0+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A5}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =(0+1)\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =1\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{addition of integers}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186609025 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*If $y$ is a vector such that $-y+w=w$ for some $w\\in V$, then $y=0$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-y+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ y+(-y+w) & =y+w &  & [\\,\\text{adding }y\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (y+(-y))+w & =y+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0+w & =y+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,] \\\\ w & =y+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom  __}\\,] \\\\ w+(-w) & =y+w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{adding }(-w)\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(-w) & =y+(w+(-w)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom  A2}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =y+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =y &  & [\\,\\text{axiom  A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot y=y \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta y)=(\\alpha \\beta )y \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:y+0=y & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )y=\\alpha y+\\beta y \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:y+(-y)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186615392 "The matrix $U$ is given below. Let ${u}_{1}$, ${u}_{2}$, and ${u}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $U.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(U).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${u}_{3}={x}_{1}{u}_{1}+{x}_{2}{u}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 7 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186615396 "The matrix $P$ is given below. Let ${p}_{1}$, ${p}_{2}$,  ${p}_{3}$ and ${p}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $P.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(P).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${p}_{4}={x}_{1}{p}_{1}+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}.$ \n\